u00b0F</strong> was the 2nd coldest November 13 low of 112 since 1892.","t_rec":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If St. Paul Island's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, St. Paul Island has set <strong>59 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 88 that chance alone would give, and <strong>137 record highs</strong>, far more than the 88 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (87). St. Paul Island's all-time high is 66\u00b0F, on August 25, 1987.","t_chg":"St. Paul Island has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 104 complete years: St. Paul Island's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.15\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.01 to +0.28), about +1.9\u00b0F since 1893. St. Paul Island's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.08 to +0.31), about +2.6\u00b0F since 1893.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in 9 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"St. Paul Island's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.08 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 24.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1964 (36.61 in); the driest, 1977 (9.82 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 1 daily record high, 3 daily record lows"}}